Sure! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives:

```js
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(' ');

  // Find the index of the first adjective (excluding "be")
  let adjectiveIndex = -1;
  for (let I = 0; I < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];
    if (word === 'be') continue; // Skip "be" as it's not an adjective
    if (/^[a-z]+$/.test(word)) { // Check if the word is only letters (an adjective)
      adjectiveIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // If there's no adjective, don't swap anything
  if (adjectiveIndex === -1) return sentence;

  // Swap the first and second adjectives
  const [firstAdjective, ...rest] = words.slice(0, adjectiveIndex + 2);
  const reversedRest = [...rest].reverse();
  const reversedSentence = [firstAdjective, ...reversedRest];

  // Join the reversed sentence back into a string
  return reversedSentence.join(' ');
}

// Example usage
console.log(switchAdjectives("Beautiful green trees are in the park"));
```

This function uses regular expressions to check whether a word is only letters (i.e., an adjective), and then finds the indices of the first two adjectives using slice(). It then creates a new array by concatenating the first adjective with the reversed second adjective and the rest of the words, and finally joins the reversed sentence back into a string. The result should be a sentence with swapped adjectives, like "Green beautiful trees are in the park".

