To implement bubble sort algorithm in C++ with minimal memory usage and error avoidance, you can follow these steps:

1. Define a function to perform bubble sort:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped = true;
    for (int I = 0; I < n - 1 && swapped; ++i) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Here, `arr` is the input array and `n` is its size. The outer loop runs `n-1` times because after each iteration, the largest element will have "bubbled up" to its correct position, so we don't need to compare it again. The inner loop compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are out of order. We keep track of whether any swaps were made during the inner loop, and if not, then the array is already sorted and we can exit the outer loop early.

