Here's a Javascript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all entries in an object where the nested key value matches the given string key:

```javascript
function getNestedValues(obj, keyPath) {
  let keys = keyPath.split(".");
  let value = obj;

  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length; i++) {
    if (!value[keys[i]]) {
      return []; // Key not found, return empty array
    }
    value = value[keys[i]];
  }

  return Object.values(value); // Return values of current object
}

// Example usage
const data = {
  user: {
    name: "John Doe",
    email: "john@example.com"
  },
  orders: [
    { id: 123, status: "shipped" },
    { id: 456, status: "pending" }
  ]
};

const result1 = getNestedValues(data, "user.name"); // Returns ["John Doe"]
const result2 = getNestedValues(data, "orders.status"); // Returns ["shipped", "pending"]
const result3 = getNestedValues(data, "nonExistentKey"); // Returns []
```

This function recursively traverses the object using the key path provided in `keyPath`, and returns an array containing the values of the last object encountered during the traversal. If any key is missing, it immediately returns an empty array. Note that this function assumes that the input `obj` is actually an object, so it will throw a TypeError if `obj` is not an object or null.

